5.90.m:
The frog said:
"In this body of water fire is currently staying;
because of him, these numerous water-born creatures living within the water are dead."

Then Brahmā, together with the gods, spoke again to the consumer of oblations:
"O purifier, become visible in the agnihotra rites of the brahmins;
now request from me your desired boon."

Sūta said:
Having spoken thus, the venerable grandfather ceased;
and the purifier also remained in the agnihotra rites of the brahmins.

5.100.m:
Having spoken thus, Rāma, desirous of going to the abode of the gods, / thought once again, remembering his friend Vibhīṣaṇa:
“Imperishable kingship in Laṅkā was given by me to him then, / being well pleased by his great devotion, for as long as the moon, sun, and stars endure.
Since the race of the rākṣasas is known to be exceedingly cruel / on this earth, especially of those nourished by boons;
if by the nature of a rākṣasa that great-souled Vibhīṣaṇa / should come into conflict with the gods, just as Rāvaṇa did,
the gods will slay him by means beginning with conciliation, / just as [they did] his brother, the Ten-necked one, the thorn of the three worlds.
Then my word would become false; therefore, having gone to his presence, / I shall give him instruction so that he does not harm the gods.
Likewise, my second supreme friend is the monkey / named Sugrīva, the highly fortunate, and Jāmbavān is another;
